course: dictionary
lesson: 7590
title: Prick: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and prick is one of them. The words around it are its context, and they tell you which meaning fits. «She felt a sharp prick as the thorn caught her finger.» Here a prick is a small sharp pain, because the thorn caught her finger and it hurt.
explain: «The baker pricked the potatoes with a fork before cooking them.» Pricked is a doing word here. The fork and the word before cooking are your clues. The baker made tiny holes in the potatoes so they would not burst. Try your meaning in the sentence: made tiny holes with a sharp point. Does it still make sense? Yes, so you have it.
explain: «The cat pricked up its ears when it heard the mouse.» Prick up is a phrase. A cat does not get pricks in its ears, so look at the clue heard. When an animal hears something, it raises its ears to listen carefully. Always ask: what would fit here? Then test your meaning in the sentence.
fact: Prick can be a verb (prick, pricks, pricked, pricking) or a noun (a prick, pricks).
fact: «Prick» means to make a tiny hole in something with a sharp point, like a needle or a thorn.
fact: «A prick» can mean a small sharp pain, or the tiny mark a sharp point leaves.
fact: «Prick up its ears» means an animal raises its ears to listen carefully.
fact: Context clues, such as a fork, a thorn or a sound, tell you which meaning of prick is right.
exercise: «She felt a sharp prick as the thorn caught her finger.» What is a prick here? | A soft, gentle touch | A rose growing in the garden | *A small sharp pain | A hole made in a wall
exercise: «The cat pricked up its ears when it heard the mouse.» What does pricked up mean? | *Raised its ears to listen carefully | Laid its ears flat in anger | Lost its ears in the long grass | Covered its ears with its paws
exercise: «Mum pricked the sausages with a fork so they would not burst.» What does pricked mean here? | Cooked them very slowly | Washed them carefully in water | Covered them in sauce | *Made tiny holes with a sharp point
test: «Ears pricked forward mean a horse is interested.» What does pricked mean here? | *Raised to listen carefully | Hidden under a blanket | Stuck with a pin | Made a tiny hole in the ground
test: «Knitting needles are kept in a pincushion so none are left to prick people or pets.» What does prick mean here? | To paint a picture | To sing loudly | *To make a tiny hole with a sharp point | To lift up something heavy
exam: Which sentence uses prick as a noun meaning a small sharp pain? | *«She felt a prick from the thorn.» | «The bread was pricked all over.» | «The dog pricked its ears.» | «She will prick the paper with a pin.»
